"""Actual contingency replacements; no savings credited for already-applied queue entries."""
CHANGES={}
def add(k,p,a,g,recall=0,sub='INTERP',ao='B',bloom='Analyse'):
 CHANGES[k]={'prompt':p,'canonical_answer':a,'marking_guidance':g.split('|'),'recall':recall,'subtype':sub,'assessment_objectives':[ao],'blooms_level':bloom}
add('9-007','State a metal that can displace copper from aqueous copper(II) sulfate.','For example magnesium, zinc or iron.','One suitable metal above copper in the reactivity series, such as magnesium, zinc or iron (1).',1,'FEATURE','A','Remember')
add('6-002','Suggest how the unchanged molecular formula before and after melting ice supports a classification of this change.','It supports a physical change: the substance remains H2O rather than forming a new substance.','Physical change supported by the unchanged identity of H2O; classification alone is insufficient (1).',0,'APP','B','Apply')
add('10-032','Suggest the structural feature of CH2=CHCH3 responsible for its rapid reaction with bromine water.','The carbon–carbon double bond (C=C).','The carbon–carbon double bond (1).',1,'FEATURE','A','Remember')
add('10-076','State the fibre class of cotton.','Natural fibre.','Natural fibre (1).',1,'FEATURE','A','Remember')
add('12-014','State the correction to a delivered-volume result if a burette reads 0.20 cm³ too high at both the initial and final readings.','No correction to the delivered-volume difference: the two equal offsets cancel.','No correction to the difference, because subtracting the readings cancels the equal +0.20 cm³ offsets (1).',0,'INTERP','C','Apply')
add('8-015','Describe the conclusion and the next test for this flame-test record. Reference: lithium gives red, calcium gives brick-red. A clean-wire blank has no colour; the sample is recorded only as “reddish”.','The record cannot distinguish lithium from calcium. Repeat the sample alongside the two reference salts under the same viewing conditions and record the shade precisely.','The vague reddish observation does not distinguish the two supplied references (1).|Repeat with both reference salts under matched viewing conditions to resolve the shade (1).')
add('8-017','Describe whether this flame test establishes sodium in a sample and how to obtain a usable result. A sodium reference gives bright yellow. The sample and the wire-only blank both give bright yellow.','It does not establish sodium from the sample: the blank already produces the same colour. Clean or replace the wire and repeat the blank until it has no yellow colour before retesting the sample.','Yellow in the blank means the sample cannot be identified from this result (1).|Obtain a non-yellow blank by cleaning/replacing the wire, then retest the sample (1).')
add('8-019','Describe what these records establish about a potassium test. Reference: potassium gives lilac and sodium gives bright yellow. On the original wire the sample looks yellow. On a cleaned wire, whose blank has no colour, a fresh portion gives lilac.','The clean-wire result supports potassium. The first yellow result does not show potassium absent; sodium contamination could have masked the lilac flame.','Lilac with a colourless blank supports potassium by the supplied reference (1).|The earlier yellow result cannot exclude potassium because sodium colour can mask lilac (1).')
add('8-021','Describe the two corrections needed to this report: “The calcium solution became brick-red, proving calcium.” The raw record says the solution stayed colourless; the flame looked brick-red; no wire blank was tested. Reference: calcium gives a brick-red flame.','Brick-red describes the flame, not the solution. The observation is consistent with calcium, but a wire blank is needed before attributing the colour to the sample.','Correct the observed object to flame; solution stayed colourless (1).|Qualify the identity claim because no blank excluded wire contamination (1).')
add('8-023','Describe which sample supports barium and why the other result is unusable. Reference: barium gives a green flame. Sample P and its wire blank both give green. Sample Q gives green but its wire blank has no colour.','Q supports barium because its colour exceeds the blank. P is inconclusive because its wire blank already gives green.','Q supported by green sample with colourless blank (1).|P inconclusive because green also occurs in its blank (1).')
add('8-037','Describe whether these observations establish ammonia and the repeat test needed. Reference: ammonia turns damp red litmus blue. In trial 1 the paper is splashed by alkaline liquid and turns blue; in trial 2 a fresh paper exposed only to gas stays red.','The splash result cannot establish ammonia because alkaline liquid can affect litmus directly. Repeat a gas-only exposure with damp red paper and a matching blank, preventing liquid spray.','The positive liquid-contact result does not identify ammonia in the gas; gas-only result was negative (1).|Repeat with damp red litmus exposed only to gas, preventing spray and using a blank (1).')
add('8-039','Describe the conclusion for each sample. Reference: carbon dioxide makes fresh limewater milky. Gas P bubbles through limewater that stays clear. Gas Q makes fresh limewater milky; an equal-time air blank stays clear.','P has no positive carbon-dioxide result; bubbles alone show gas flow, not identity. Q supports carbon dioxide because milkiness occurs beyond the blank response.','P: bubbling without milkiness does not identify carbon dioxide (1).|Q: milkiness with a clear blank supports carbon dioxide (1).')
add('8-041','Describe the identity supported by this test and the decisive evidence. References: an acidic gas can turn damp blue litmus red; chlorine then bleaches its colour. A sample turns the paper red at 2 s and white at 15 s; a damp-paper air blank stays blue.','The record supports chlorine. The later bleaching beyond the air blank is decisive; the initial red colour alone would show acidity, not uniquely chlorine.','Chlorine supported by the red-then-bleached sequence and unchanged blank (1).|Distinguish the decisive bleaching from the non-specific initial acidic response (1).')
add('8-043','Describe why a negative hydrogen identification is unjustified and how to repeat the test. Valid-test reference: a small separately collected hydrogen sample gives a squeaky pop with a lighted splint. A pupil used an extinguished splint on a small collected sample and heard no sound.','An extinguished splint does not provide the ignition used by the reference test, so no sound cannot exclude hydrogen. Repeat on a small separately collected sample with a lighted splint under teacher supervision.','No ignition source means the negative result is not a valid hydrogen test (1).|Repeat on a small separately collected sample with a lighted splint under supervision (1).')
add('8-045','Describe whether these results establish oxygen and the next test needed. Reference: oxygen relights a glowing splint. An already flaming splint keeps burning in the sample and in room air.','Continued burning in both conditions does not demonstrate the stated oxygen test. Repeat with a glowing, non-flaming splint in the sample and compare with an air control, looking for relighting.','Existing flames continuing in both sample and air do not establish the reference positive result (1).|Use a glowing non-flaming splint and air comparison, recording whether it relights (1).')
add('11-020','Explain which fertiliser best corrects the measured shortage without adding a nutrient already in excess. Soil tests: nitrogen adequate, phosphorus low, potassium excessive. Fertiliser A supplies N and K; B supplies P; C supplies N, P and K.','B supplies the deficient phosphorus. It avoids the additional potassium in A and C, which the soil already has in excess.','Select B because it supplies the measured phosphorus deficiency (1).|Reject potassium-containing A/C because potassium is already excessive (1).',0,'APP','B','Apply')
def apply(x,key):
 c=CHANGES.get(key)
 if c is None:return None
 for f,v in c.items():
  if f!='recall':x[f]=v
 return c['recall']
